          "thinking": "Now I understand the codebase. Let me analyze what needs to be done:\n\n1. Add an opt-in option `--max-pending-hours H` to `python3 record/record.py check-anchors`\n2. Without the option, behavior is unchanged: pending proofs are still accepted (exit 0)\n3. With the option, if any range head's proof is still pending and its Sigstore attestation time is more than H hours ago, exit 1\n4. Print to stderr a message containing `still pending N h`, where N is the whole number of hours since the attestation\n5. A pending proof younger than H hours is fine (exit 0)\n6. All existing tests must still pass\n\nLooking at the `cmd_check_anchors` function, I see:\n- It loads the chain\n- It iterates through anchors, checking both `.ots` (OpenTimestamps) and `.sigstore.json` files\n- For `.ots` files, it calls `check_ots` which returns the earliest Bitcoin block time or `None` if pending\n- For `.sigstore.json` files, it calls `check_sigstore` which returns `t` (integrated_time), predicate, and commit\n- Range heads are checked to have their `.ots` proofs\n\nThe key insight: I need to track for each range head (which has an OTS proof), what the Sigstore attestation time was, and if the OTS proof is still pending (returns `None`), check if the attestation time is more than H hours ago.\n\nLet me trace through the code:\n1. `ranges` list contains tuples of `(from_seq, to_seq, commit)` for each sigstore anchor\n2. `stamped` set contains seq numbers that have `.ots` files\n3. For each range head `hi`, it checks if `hi in stamped`\n4. The `check_ots` function returns `None` if the proof is still pending\n\nI need to:\n1. Add the `--max-pending-hours` argument to the `check-anchors` subparser\n2. In `cmd_check_anchors`, track the integrated_time for each range head (from the sigstore check)\n3. When checking OTS proofs for range heads, if the proof is pending (returns `None`) and `--max-pending-hours` is set, calculate hours since attestation and check against the limit\n\nLet me look more carefully at the data flow:\n- When processing `.sigstore.json` anchors, we get `t` (integrated_time) and the range `(from_seq, to_seq)`\n- The range head is `to_seq` (which is `seq`)\n- We store ranges as `(from_seq, to_seq, commit)` but we also need the integrated_time\n- When processing `.ots` anchors, we check if the proof is pending\n\nSo I need to modify the code to:\n1. Store the integrated_time along with the range info\n2. After collecting all ranges, check each range head's OTS proof status\n3. If pending and `--max-pending-hours` is set, compare current time vs integrated_time\n\nActually, looking more carefully, the `check_ots` is called when processing `.ots` files in the first loop. The `ranges` are collected from `.sigstore.json` files. The check for missing `.ots` files happens in a separate loop after.\n\nI think the best approach is:\n1. In the first loop, when we process `.sigstore.json`, store the integrated_time with the range\n2. In the first loop, when we process `.ots`, we already call `check_ots` and get the result (timestamp or None for pending)\n3. We need to correlate the OTS proof status with the range head's attestation time\n\nLet me restructure the logic:\n- Track for each seq: the OTS proof status (confirmed time or pending) and the sigstore attestation time (if it's a range head)\n- After processing all anchors, check each range head: if its OTS proof is pending and attestation time > H hours ago, report error\n\nLet me implement this step by step.\n\nFirst, let me check the test that stubs `check_sigstore` and `check_ots` to understand the expected behavior better.\n\nLooking at `test_a_missing_ots_proof_for_a_range_head_is_caught`:\n- It creates 2 entries\n- Creates a sigstore anchor for entry 2 (range 1-2)\n- Stubs `check_sigstore` to return `(1, range_predicate(chain, 1, 2), \"c0ffee\")` - note the first return value is `t=1` (integrated_time)\n- Stubs `check_ots` to return `None` (pending)\n- Expects exit code 1 with \"000002.ots is missing\"\n\nWait, the stub for `check_sigstore` returns `(1, predicate, commit)` where `1` is the integrated_time. And `check_ots` returns `None` for pending.\n\nSo in the current code, when an OTS proof is missing, it's caught. But when an OTS proof exists but is pending (returns `None`), it's currently accepted.\n\nNow I need to add the `--max-pending-hours` option to make pending proofs older than H hours cause an error.\n\nLet me implement this:\n\n1. Add `--max-pending-hours` argument to the check-anchors parser\n2. In `cmd_check_anchors`:\n   - Track integrated_time for each range head (from sigstore anchors)\n   - Track OTS proof status for each seq (from ots anchors)\n   - After processing all anchors, for each range head:\n     - If OTS proof is pending (None) and integrated_time is known and `--max-pending-hours` is set:\n       - Calculate hours since attestation\n       - If > H hours, add to bad list with \"still pending N h\" message\n3. Print the bad messages to stderr (already done in the existing code)\n4. Return 1 if bad, else 2 if cant, else 0\n\nLet me write the code changes.",
